<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Eridu</h1><p class="st-meta">5400 BCE – 2600 BCE (approx.) · capital Eridu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the oldest settlements in southern Mesopotamia, remembered by the Sumerians as the first city to receive kingship and as the home of the water god Enki.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Eridu was settled around 5400 BCE on a sandy rise at the edge of the marshes near the ancient shore of the Gulf, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a>. Excavations by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuad-safar">Fuad Safar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seton-lloyd">Seton Lloyd</a> in 1946 to 1949 uncovered a sequence of some eighteen superimposed temples, growing from a single mud-brick room into a monumental platform shrine.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was above all a sacred place, the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> (Akkadian Ea), god of fresh water, crafts and wisdom, whose temple was called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-abzu">E-abzu</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> opens with the words that kingship first descended from heaven at Eridu, naming the legendary kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alulim">Alulim</a> and Alalngar. Its population was small compared with nearby Ur, and little is known of its secular government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a> Eridu had lost political weight to Ur, though it remained a revered cult centre; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> and his successors of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> there. The site was largely abandoned by the mid-first millennium BCE, but its myths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> and the first city endured in Mesopotamian literature.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eridu.jpg" alt="Eridu" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziggurat_at_Eridu_(30809118442).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>5400 BCE – 2600 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd>55th century BCE – <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eridu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sumerian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sumerian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eridug</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-4000&amp;focus=eridu" aria-label="Eridu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5871 1054 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eridu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6191 1310h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6191 1310h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-4000&amp;focus=eridu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

